  "provider_query": "English Wikipedia HTTP article: the default TCP port",
  "answer": "The English Wikipedia **HTTP** article states that unencrypted HTTP typically uses **TCP port 80**.\n\nFrom the article (Data exchange): TCP/IP connections use well-known ports—**typically port 80** if unencrypted, or port 443 if encrypted. The example request–response is for `www.example.com` on **port 80**. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP rather than TCP.\n\nConcise related sources:\n\n1. **HTTP – Wikipedia** — typically port 80 (unencrypted); example uses port 80. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP  \n2. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers – Wikipedia** — port 80 assigned to HTTP (TCP in HTTP/1.x and HTTP/2). https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers  \n3. **Port (computer networking) – Wikipedia** — HTTP uses port 80 by default in URLs when no port is given. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)  \n4. **Port 80 – Wikipedia** (disambiguation) — TCP port 80 is most often used by HTTP. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_80  \n5. **IANA service name and port registry** — `http` / `www` on **80/tcp** (World Wide Web HTTP; RFC 9110). https://www.iana.org/assignments/service-names-port-numbers  \n6. **MDN Glossary: Port** — default port for HTTP is 80. https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Port  \n7. **Stack Overflow** — default HTTP port is 80; omit it in the URL unless non-default. https://stackoverflow.com/questions/50888270/why-do-we-need-to-specify-a-port-number-while-using-http-protocol  \n8. **Super User** — historical assignment: Tim Berners-Lee assumed port 80; later formalized as `www`/`http` on 80. https://superuser.com/questions/996828/why-was-port-80-chosen-as-the-default-http-port-and-443-as-the-default-https-por",
